+# R48 phase 1 — Q5_K mat-vec decode
+
+**Inherited:** `9ff4644b1dee75c3d1040c91ae306eb8388e65c0`, worktree `C:/ArifiLabs/cache/r48-kernels-wt`,
+branch `lane-234/r48-matvec-kernels-2026-09-14`, status clean apart from the untracked prompt file.
+No commit, no branch move, no upstream operation was performed. Nothing was compiled and nothing was
+run on the GPU — the measurement chain owns the box.
+
+---
+
+## 1. The brief was partly stale. Read this section before paying for anything else.
+
+Three of the five attack items in the brief describe properties `mul_mat_vec_q5_k.comp` **already
+had** at `9ff4644b1`. This is the highest-value output of the phase, because it stops HQ paying a
+second time for work that is already in the tree.
+
+| Brief item | Status at inherited SHA | Evidence |
+|---|---|---|
+| (a) decode a whole superblock per thread into registers, reuse across `NUM_COLS` | **Already done.** The weight decode sat above the `for j < NUM_COLS` loop; every column already shared one decode. | old `mul_mat_vec_q5_k.comp:15-79` |
+| (b) `get_scale_min_k4` once per sub-block, not per column | **Already done, and the function is not called at all.** A vectorized 6-bit unpack replaced it, hoisted above the column loop. | old `:19-35` |
+| (a′) `qh` decoded per element | **Already false.** `qh` arrives as one packed `u32` and is applied to four weights at a time by mask. | old `:45-55` |
+| (c) coalesced 16-byte loads of `qs`/`qh` | **Not a drop-in, and I did not force it.** See §5. | old `:37-45` |
+| (d) `NUM_COLS` column blocking so widths 2..8 share every weight load | **Already done for the WEIGHT side. Was NOT done for the ACTIVATION side.** That gap is the change. | §2 |
+| (e) route Q5_K to f32 unless the int path wins | **Already in force for `n > 1`.** `n == 1` was the only width left on MMVQ. | `ggml-vulkan.cpp:11801` |
+
+### The routing fact that reframes the phase (CHECKED)
+
+On AMD, `ggml_vk_should_use_mmvq()` **already returns false for Q5_K at every `n > 1`**
+(`ggml-vulkan.cpp:11801-11803`). `n == 1` is not covered by that block, falls through to the vendor
+switch, and AMD at `k >= 2048` returns `true`. So the live Q5_K decode route on this box is **split**:
+
+| width | shader |
+|---|---|
+| `n = 1` | `mul_mat_vecq.comp` + the Q5_K arm of `mul_mat_vecq_funcs.glsl` (q8_1 integer dot) |
+| `n = 2..8` | `mul_mat_vec_q5_k.comp` (f32 dequant) |
+
+`q6_K` returns `false` on every non-Intel vendor (`:11724`), so it **never enters the MMVQ framework**.
+That is the plain explanation for the brief's observation that q6_K rows "stay flat at 79-87%" while
+Q5_K steps: they are not being measured through the same framework.
+
+**This breaks the briefed acceptance rule, and HQ must be told before the chain runs.** ">= +10%
+median at `n=1`" measures the MMVQ shader; "widths 4..8 within 1.25x of width 1" measures the f32
+shader. Under default routing those are two different kernels, so **no single change can satisfy
+both**. §7 gives a two-arm pairing plan instead.
+
+---
+
+## 2. The change, and why it reaches the part of the traffic that scales with width
+
+The weight side of `mul_mat_vec_q5_k.comp` was already shared across columns. The **activation** side
+was not. With the row loop outermost, the eight `vec2` loads of `data_b` were issued `num_rows` times
+per superblock per column, for bytes that do not depend on the row at all.
+
+`rm_kq = 2` on this box (CHECKED: `ggml-vulkan.cpp:6860`, raised to 4 only under `AMD_GCN`; the 780M
+is RDNA3, and the `rm_int_n`/`rm_id` RDNA3 overrides apply to the q8_1 and `_id` pipelines, not to the
+f32 dmmv registration at `:7104`). So `NUM_ROWS = 2`, and the activation loads were issued exactly
+twice as often as needed.
+
+### Bytes and threads per dispatch (DERIVED from the block layout, not measured)
+
+One superblock = 256 weights = 176 bytes, worked by 16 cooperating threads.
+
+| side | per thread per row | ×16 threads | note |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `dm` | 4 B | | |
+| `scales` (3 × u32) | 12 B | | |
+| `qs` (4 × u16) | 8 B | | |
+| `qh` (2 × u16) | 4 B | | |
+| **A total** | **28 B** | **448 B** | against a 176 B block — 2.5x *request* over-read, but the same cache lines, so **bus** traffic stays 176 B |
+| **B (per column)** | 64 B | **1024 B** | = exactly the 256 activations of that superblock |
+
+Per superblock, per column, the activation requests go from `NUM_ROWS × 1024 B = 2048 B` to `1024 B`.
+The weight term is unchanged at `NUM_ROWS × 448 B`. At `n = 8` that is 16384 B → 8192 B of activation
+requests against a fixed 896 B of weight requests: **the term that grows with width is halved, and the
+term that does not is untouched.** That is the mechanism against the per-width step.
+
+### Honest limit on the "reaches the bus" claim
+
+I will not claim this raises the `n=1` bus rate, and HQ should not expect it to:
+
+- At default routing `n = 1` does **not use this shader at all** — it uses MMVQ. The hoist changes
+  nothing at `n = 1` unless `GGML_ARIFI_Q5K_MMVQ_N1=0` is set.
+- The weights are the only genuinely cold bytes (5.41 GiB streamed once per token). The activations
+  are `5120 × 4 B × NUM_COLS` = 20 KB per column, ~160 KB at `n=8` — L2-resident, not bus traffic.
+  Halving activation *requests* cuts issue pressure and L2 traffic, **not DRAM bytes**.
+- So this change targets the **per-width step**, which is an issue/ALU-bound effect. It is not a
+  mechanism for moving the 84%-of-best figure at `n=1`. The brief's "1.25x of width 1" target
+  presumes bus-bound behaviour at width 8; if the shader is ALU-bound there, part of the rise is
+  arithmetic and not a defect. **I could not test which, and HQ should read the Q4_K control row to
+  find out** — Q4_K is 96-100% of best and shares the reduction, so its own width curve is the
+  reference for how much rise is structural.
+
+### Why a specialization constant and not an unconditional rewrite
+
+The hoisted arm holds the decoded weights of **all** `num_rows` rows live across the whole column loop
+(`NUM_ROWS × 26` floats) instead of one row's worth. That is precisely the shape that spilled in
+lane-230: `mul_mat_vec_sx8.comp:60-71` records a whole-block decode losing **-45% at n=5 and -77% at
+n=8** once live state grew with the batch. A kernel written without a compiler must not bet a default
+on that. One binary serves both arms, exactly as `GGML_VK_Q6K_DIRECT_SCALES` already does.
+
+**Both arms call the same `decode_superblock()` and `accumulate_column()`.** They are bit-identical by
+construction rather than by inspection: the fma chains, their operand order, and the accumulation
+order into `temp[j][n]` are one piece of code. The constant changes only how many times the `data_b`
+loads issue. No expression in the decode was rewritten — it was lifted verbatim.
+
+---
+
+## 3. Two things I deliberately did NOT do, with the evidence
+
+**I did not port the S-X8 whole-block pattern to Q5_K.** `mul_mat_vec_sx8.comp:60-71` records that
+whole-block ownership spills at `NUM_COLS >= 4` and was gated to `n <= 3`. Q5_K's live f32 range is
+exactly `n = 2..8` — the range where that pattern is documented to lose.
+
+**I did not rewrite the MMVQ `iter()` loop nest, but I found and am banking a real defect there.**
+In `mul_mat_vecq.comp:37-78` the nest is `for j (NUM_COLS) { load B; for n { mmvq_dot_product() } }`.
+`mmvq_dot_product()` performs the **entire A-side decode** — `repack4()` plus `get_dm_scale()`, ~8
+loads and the full 6-bit scale unpack (`mul_mat_vecq_funcs.glsl:370-435`). Because it sits inside the
+`j` loop, **the weights are re-decoded once per column**: a `NUM_COLS`-fold redundancy, for every
+MMVQ quant type, not just Q5_K.
+
+This is a candidate mechanism for the **unexplained `NUM_COLS == 7` tail defect** recorded in
+`tools/arifi-sync/protected-wins.json` row 58 ("points at a `NUM_COLS==7` tail defect in the mmvq
+framework rather than a tuning loss; Commit D or a narrowed admit starts there"). Fixing it means
+decoding A for all `num_rows` first — live state `NUM_ROWS × (i32vec4 + vec2)`, which does **not**
+grow with `NUM_COLS` — then looping `j`.
+
+I did not do it this phase for two reasons: Q5_K's only MMVQ width is `n = 1`, where the redundancy
+factor is 1, so it buys **Q5_K nothing**; and the blast radius is every MMVQ type including several
+with protected wins. It is a separate lane with its own pairing. **Receipt banked here so it is not
+re-derived:** `mul_mat_vecq.comp:37` (nest), `mul_mat_vecq_funcs.glsl:423-435` (the per-call decode).
+
+---
+
+## 4. Changed files
+
+| File | Change |
+|---|---|
+| `ggml/src/ggml-vulkan/vulkan-shaders/mul_mat_vec_q5_k.comp` | Decode and accumulate factored into `decode_superblock()` / `accumulate_column()` (verbatim lift, no expression rewritten). `calc_superblock()` gains two arms under new spec constant 3 `Q5K_B_HOIST`: arm 0 = shipped order, arm 1 = activation loaded once per superblock and reused across rows. |
